// A SQL database, as the vocabulary's `database` kind at `type: sql`.
//
// # Four types, four packages
//
// `database` is one kind with four data models, and they share nothing but a
// name: `sql` speaks statements, `kv` speaks keys, `document` speaks JSON, and
// `vector` speaks embeddings. Folding them into one interface would be four
// interfaces wearing a trenchcoat — and would make a guest that needs a
// keystore carry a SQL value variant it will never construct.
//
// # It is shaped like the client the author already uses
//
// `query` answers rows, `exec` answers what it changed. That is Go's
// `database/sql`, Python's DBAPI, node-postgres and every JDBC wrapper — so
// bindings over this are a thin adapter rather than a new idiom to learn. It is
// deliberately NOT an ORM and not a query builder: those belong in the guest's
// own ecosystem, over this.
//
// # Two verbs, and `exec` is a write because the host cannot classify SQL
//
// The vocabulary says so where it declares them: nothing here parses a
// statement to decide whether it changes anything, so an author who asked for
// `query` gets reads and an author who needs to write asks for `exec`.
//
// # A row is flat on purpose
//
// `values` is ROW-MAJOR with `columns` giving the width, rather than a list of
// lists. Nesting a list of variants inside a list inside a record is three
// composites deep in the canonical ABI, and the flat shape carries exactly the
// same information for a guest that already has the column count. Chunking it
// back into rows is one line in any language's bindings.
//
// # What is not here, and what it would take
//
// TRANSACTIONS. `begin`/`commit`/`rollback` need a handle that outlives a call,
// which is a WIT `resource` — the shape is available and the missing half is
// underneath: what backs this must be able to pin a connection for the life of
// one, and an adapter that pools per call cannot. It is a change to the backing
// before it is a change to this file.
//
// PREPARED STATEMENTS, for the same reason and with less to gain: `args` is
// already parameterised, so what a prepare adds is plan reuse across calls, and
// an instance that serves one request at a time gets most of that from the
// server's own statement cache.
//
// A CURSOR. `query` answers the whole result set as one value, so a statement
// that selects a million rows materialises them in the guest's memory. SQL's own
// answer to that is `LIMIT`/`OFFSET`, which a caller writes today and which
// every engine here supports — a streaming cursor is a `resource` and wants the
// same pinned connection a transaction does.
package taubyte:sql@0.1.0;

interface store {
    // A SQL value, in and out. NULL is its own case rather than an absent
    // option, because a column that IS null and a column that was not selected
    // are different answers and a guest has to be able to tell them apart.
    variant value {
        null,
        text(string),
        integer(s64),
        real(f64),
        boolean(bool),
        blob(list<u8>),
    }

    record rows {
        columns: list<string>,
        // Row-major: columns.len() values per row. See the package doc.
        values: list<value>,
    }

    // What a write changed.
    //
    // `last-insert-id` IS 0 WHEN THE SERVER DID NOT REPORT ONE, which is most
    // of them: it is a MySQL and SQLite idea, and PostgreSQL answers the same
    // question with `RETURNING`. A guest that needs the id portably writes the
    // RETURNING and reads it out of `query`.
    record outcome {
        affected: s64,
        last-insert-id: s64,
    }

    variant error {
        // The label is not in this resource's `database:` block.
        no-such-database(string),
        // Declared, but not with this verb — or the target is on an engine
        // this cloud has no adapter registered for.
        denied(string),
        // The server could not be reached.
        unavailable(string),
        // The server answered, and what it said was no: a syntax error, a
        // constraint, a permission. It is ITS OWN ARM because a caller retries
        // an outage and fixes a statement.
        failed(string),
    }

    query: func(name: string, statement: string, args: list<value>) -> result<rows, error>;
    exec: func(name: string, statement: string, args: list<value>) -> result<outcome, error>;
}

world imports {
    import store;
}
